'' A NON-TRIVIAL UDT RESULT THROUGH A PROCEDURE POINTER compiles, links and runs. A call
'' through a pointer passed NO hidden result slot, while the pointer's signature
'' (PtrCallSigIndex_) let the emitter infer one from the result's size:
''
''   - a 24-byte DWSTRING, as PsTooltip's 'pTip->TooltipCallback( hWin, hAttached, idx )'
''     returns, put hWin in the slot and the LONG idx where a pointer belongs -- clang
''     refused PsCalendar: "'%t30' defined with type 'i32' but expected 'ptr'";
''   - a 4-byte result WITH A DESTRUCTOR came back "in a register" the callee never
''     wrote, and printed garbage.
''
'' fbc passes the slot first either way, measured: '(*cb)( &TMP$7$2, a, b, idx )'. Now
'' the pointer's signature takes it as a named procedure's does (SignatureFromProc), and
'' the call site passes it (SretCallType_). Plain-data results were already right.
''
'' A DRIVER TEST BECAUSE IT HAS TO LINK: --dump-ir stops before clang, and the failure
'' was clang's. The unfixed compiler fails this exact way on the source; the source is a
'' goldenrun.sh differential too -- built and run by fbc and by tiko, byte-identical.
'' TEST: cli
'' ARGS: -target win64-msvc -i toolchains/tiko-windows/inc tests/driver/src/proc-ptr-sret.bas -o tests/driver/procptrsret.exe
'' ENV: TKO_LIBPATH=toolchains/tiko-windows/lib/win64-msvc
'' EXPECT-EXIT: 0
'' EXPECT-FILE: tests/driver/procptrsret.exe
